  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1257 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWhile high pressure continues to build over the Panhandles there \nwill still be some lingering colder air from the departed system. \nResidual low clouds will still be a concern as well, that will hold \nMonday's highs back into the mid 40s to lower 50s. The northwest may \nclear out first and therefore it could be a bit warmer there, but \nthe southeast should still be cooler and cloudy.  Winds will be a \nbit breezy as well on Monday and even more so on Tuesday. Cross \nbarrier flow will pick up a bit from the ridge shifting east as the \nstrong jet aloft still remains further west near the Four Corners. \nBut this will be enough for a weak lee surface low to set up on \nMonday and strengthen a bit on Tuesday.  Winds may gust up around 40 \nmph in the northwest on Tuesday with milder, yet still slightly \nbelow normal highs.  On Wednesday we'll have mild southwest flow \naloft over the Panhandles with an approaching shortwave. Right now \nthe precipitation that we get out of this shortwave is very much up \nin the air as a lot of it is eroded away after it crosses the Rockies \nand enters some drier air, and it looks like limited Gulf moisture \nwill be brought up to the Panhandles with that shortwave.  So pops \nare on the light side mid to late next week.  \n\nAs the system exits on Wednesday the high will reposition itself \nover Mexico but will be suppressed a bit by that last system, but \nthis will keep a mild southwest flow over the Panhandles and a \nreturn of temperatures back in the 60s.  There is another Pacific \nweather system that progged to reach the Panhandles next Friday, and \nout ahead of it there's a low chance for some showers/storms on \nThursday in the southeast and again on Friday as the system move \nacross the Panhandles.  As we get closer we'll look into this system \nto see if it's more of a rain producer, or a dry slot breezy system. \n \nWeber",
